@page "/rules"
@implements IDisposable
@inject RulebookReader Rulebook
@inject Commands Commands

@* Looking a rule up, which is the thing that happens every session rather than once.

   **The search admits what it did and did not find, and that is most of the design.** A search
   that always shows its five best rows reads as five answers however carefully the caution is
   worded — so a passage that matched only inside its body says so on the row, and a query the
   book has nothing for says that in as many words rather than showing something adjacent.

   **Every result carries its printed page**, because a rules answer that can be checked against
   the copy on the table is worth more than one that cannot. That is the difference between this
   and remembering. *@

<PageTitle>Rules reference — Prowlers &amp; Paragons</PageTitle>

<h1>Rules reference</h1>

@if (_refused)
{
    <Panel Title="Sign in first">
        <p>Reading the book needs an account. <a href="signin">Sign in</a>, then come back.</p>
    </Panel>
}
else
{
    <Panel Title="Search the book" Aside="@Held">
        <Field Label="What are you looking for?" For="rules-search">
            @* A form, so Enter searches — which is what somebody typing a question expects, and
               the only way this is usable one-handed at a table. *@
            <form @onsubmit="Search" @onsubmit:preventDefault>
                <div class="inline">
                    <input id="rules-search" type="search" @bind="_query" @bind:event="oninput"
                           placeholder="knockback, trait cap, how surprise works" />
                    <button type="submit" class="btn primary" disabled="@_searching">Search</button>
                </div>
            </form>
        </Field>

        @* One pattern rather than a nested `if`: this asks a single question — did a search run and
           come back with nothing — and splitting it across two conditions reads as two. *@
        @if (_results is { Found: 0 })
        {
            <EmptyState>@NothingFound</EmptyState>
        }
    </Panel>

    @* The results are a panel of their own, and what was found is its aside.

       They sat unframed between two panels, on the page's own ground — which read as an
       unfinished section rather than as the answer to the box above. Seen in a proof; no test
       could have, since the rows and the sentence were all present and correct. *@
    @if (_results is { Found: > 0 } found)
    {
        <Panel Title="@Answering" Aside="@Summary(found)">
            <ChosenList>
                @foreach (var result in found.Results)
                {
                    var key = (result.Chapter, result.Index);
                    <ChosenRow Cost="@RulebookCitation.For(result)">
                        <Body>
                            <div>
                                <b>@result.Heading</b>
                                @if (!result.MatchedHeading)
                                {
                                    @* Said on the row rather than once at the top: a reader scanning
                                       a list is deciding about one row at a time. *@
                                    <span class="tag">mentioned in passing</span>
                                }
                            </div>
                            <div class="small muted">@result.Snippet</div>

                            @if (_open == key)
                            {
                                @if (_passage is { } passage)
                                {
                                    @* The same component the Power editor's disclosure uses —
                                       one idiom for the book's own words, not a second one for the
                                       same text on another page. It was two hand-written copies of
                                       this markup, which is what that comment used to promise and
                                       could not enforce. *@
                                    <BookText Text="@passage.Text"
                                              Citation="@($"{passage.SourceRef}, printed p.{passage.PrintedPage}")" />
                                }
                                else
                                {
                                    <p class="small muted">Fetching it…</p>
                                }
                            }
                        </Body>
                        <Actions>
                            <button type="button" class="btn small"
                                    aria-expanded="@(_open == key ? "true" : "false")"
                                    @onclick="() => Toggle(result)">
                                @(_open == key ? "Close" : "Read it")
                            </button>
                        </Actions>
                    </ChosenRow>
                }
            </ChosenList>
        </Panel>
    }

    @* The chapters, as the controls they always looked like.

       **The passage counts are gone.** "742 passages" is a statistic about how the extractor
       split the text — the app describing its own internals to somebody who asked about a
       rulebook. What is left is the two things a reader can act on: the chapter's name, and where
       it sits in a physical copy.

       **A row cannot search for nothing, so it is disabled until the box holds something.** The
       server would answer an empty query with `found: 0`, and this page prints that as a sentence
       about the book — so a row that ran on an empty box would tell a reader Ch.4 has nothing to
       say, on the strength of their not having typed yet. The aside says what the rows want
       instead; the alternative, running the chapter's own title as the query, is worse and the
       entry in PROGRESS.md says why. *@
    @if (_contents is { } contents)
    {
        <Panel Title="What is here" Aside="@Scoping">
            <ChosenList>
                @foreach (var chapter in contents.Chapters)
                {
                    <ChosenRow Cost="@Pages(chapter)">
                        <Body>
                            <div><b>@chapter.Title</b></div>
                            <div class="small muted">@chapter.SourceRef</div>
                        </Body>
                        <Actions>
                            <button type="button" class="btn small" disabled="@(!CanScope)"
                                    @onclick="() => SearchIn(chapter)">
                                Search this chapter
                            </button>
                        </Actions>
                    </ChosenRow>
                }
            </ChosenList>
        </Panel>
    }
}

@code {
    private string _query = "";
    private bool _searching;
    private bool _refused;

    private RulebookResults? _results;
    private RulebookContents? _contents;

    private (int Chapter, int Index)? _open;
    private RulebookPassage? _passage;

    /// <summary>
    /// Which chapter the results on screen came from, or null for the whole book.
    ///
    /// <para>Kept so the answer can say where it looked. A narrowed list presented under the same
    /// heading as a whole-book one is the "looks like a list of links and is not one" complaint in
    /// a new spelling — it would read as the book having only these three passages.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private RulebookChapter? _scope;

    /// <summary>
    /// What there is to read, asked once.
    ///
    /// <para>It doubles as the check on whether this account may read anything at all: the server
    /// refuses every address under the book's prefix the same way, so one refusal here is the
    /// answer for all four. Asking a second time to find out would be asking the same question
    /// twice and believing the second one.</para>
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para><b>A query arriving from the palette is run here, once.</b> Choosing a passage in the
    /// palette hands the reader's own words over through <see cref="Commands.RequestSearch"/> and
    /// comes here — the same idiom that hands a Power to the editor on another step, and for the
    /// same reason: this is the page that can actually read a passage. It is <i>taken</i> rather
    /// than peeked at, so it runs on arrival and never again; left set it would re-run the
    /// palette's question over whatever had since been typed into the box.</para>
    ///
    /// <para><b>Taken on arrival <i>and</i> whenever one is raised, and the second half is not
    /// belt and braces.</b> Reading it here alone was a defect: a reader already on
    /// <c>/rules</c> who opened the palette and chose a passage got nothing at all, because
    /// <c>NavigateTo("rules")</c> from <c>/rules</c> is a no-op and Blazor reuses this instance
    /// rather than initialising a second one — so the request sat in the service and fired on some
    /// later visit instead, answering a question asked minutes ago.</para>
    ///
    /// <para><b>It is asked for after the refusal is known, and skipped when the book is
    /// refused.</b> Running a search this account cannot make would answer null, which this page
    /// draws as nothing at all — beside a panel already saying to sign in first. Taken and thrown
    /// away in that case rather than left, or it would sit waiting to fire on a later visit.</para>
    /// </remarks>
    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        _contents = await Rulebook.ContentsAsync();
        _refused = _contents is null;

        // Subscribed after the fetch above rather than before it, so this handler never runs
        // against a half-initialised page — and nothing is missed by waiting, because the line
        // below takes whatever was asked for while the contents were on the wire.
        Commands.Changed += Requested;

        await TakeRequestedAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The palette closed, or something was chosen in it. If it was a passage, this is the page
    /// that answers.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <b>Dispatched, and it asks for a redraw.</b> The event is raised by whatever closed the
    /// palette — including the key listener, which arrives from the browser rather than from
    /// Blazor — and nothing else on this page is re-rendering for it.
    /// </remarks>
    private void Requested() => _ = InvokeAsync(async () =>
    {
        await TakeRequestedAsync();
        StateHasChanged();
    });

    /// <summary>
    /// Take the palette's question, if there is one, and answer it.
    ///
    /// <para>Taken before the refusal is checked, so a request made by a reader the book is not
    /// open to is cleared rather than left waiting for a visit that can run it.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task TakeRequestedAsync()
    {
        var asked = Commands.TakeRequestedSearch();

        if (_refused || string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(asked)) return;

        _query = asked;
        await RunAsync(null);
    }

    public void Dispose() => Commands.Changed -= Requested;

    private string Held => _contents is { } contents
        ? Counted(contents.Chapters.Count, "chapter", "chapters")
        : "";

    /// <summary>Whether there is anything to search for yet.</summary>
    private bool CanScope => _query.Trim().Length > 0;

    /// <summary>
    /// What the chapter rows are waiting for, or nothing when they are ready.
    ///
    /// <para>A disabled control that does not say why is a dead end. This is the panel's aside
    /// rather than a paragraph because it is a label on a state, not prose about the feature.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private string Scoping => CanScope ? "" : "type a word above first";

    /// <summary>The chapter's printed page range, which is how to find it in a paper copy.</summary>
    private static string Pages(RulebookChapter chapter) => chapter.PrintedPages switch
    {
        [var only] => FormattableString.Invariant($"p.{only}"),
        [var first, .., var last] => FormattableString.Invariant($"pp.{first}–{last}"),
        _ => "",
    };

    /// <summary>Which book the results came out of, said above them rather than assumed.</summary>
    private string Answering => _scope is { } chapter
        ? FormattableString.Invariant($"What Ch.{chapter.Chapter} says")
        : "What the book says";

    /// <summary>
    /// The box's own search, which is always the whole book.
    ///
    /// <para><b>It clears the scope, and that is the way back.</b> A narrowing that survived the
    /// next query would answer a new question out of a chapter chosen for the old one, and nothing
    /// on screen would be wrong — which is the shape of every fault this page is built against.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private Task Search() => RunAsync(null);

    /// <summary>The same search, narrowed to one chapter by the server.</summary>
    private Task SearchIn(RulebookChapter chapter) => RunAsync(chapter);

    private async Task RunAsync(RulebookChapter? scope)
    {
        var query = _query.Trim();

        if (query.Length == 0)
        {
            _results = null;
            _scope = null;
            return;
        }

        _searching = true;
        _scope = scope;
        _open = null;
        _passage = null;

        _results = await Rulebook.SearchAsync(query, scope?.Chapter);

        _searching = false;
    }

    private async Task Toggle(RulebookResult result)
    {
        var key = (result.Chapter, result.Index);

        if (_open == key)
        {
            _open = null;
            _passage = null;
            return;
        }

        _open = key;
        _passage = null;
        _passage = await Rulebook.PassageAsync(result.Chapter, result.Index);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// What was found, in the reader's terms.
    ///
    /// <para><b>It says how many matched and how many are shown, and those differ.</b> The server
    /// caps a response; a count of what was listed presented as a count of what exists would tell
    /// a reader the book says less than it does.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private static string Summary(RulebookResults results)
    {
        var found = Counted(results.Found, "passage", "passages");
        var shown = results.Results.Count < results.Found
            ? $", best {results.Results.Count} first"
            : "";

        // No full stop: this is the panel's aside now, sitting beside "2 chapters" and
        // "3 passages", and a lone sentence-ending mark among labels reads as a typo.
        var how = results.NothingMatchedByHeading
            ? " — all mentions, no entry of its own"
            : "";

        return $"{found}{shown}{how}";
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// What to say when the book has nothing, which is a real answer and not a failure.
    ///
    /// <para>It names the words actually searched on, because filler is dropped and a reader who
    /// typed six words and was told nothing matched deserves to know which two were used.</para>
    ///
    /// <para><b>A narrowed miss says it was narrowed, and points at the way out.</b> "Nothing in
    /// the book uses knockback" is false when only one chapter was read, and it is the exact
    /// over-claim this page exists to avoid — so the scoped wording names the chapter and the
    /// control that widens it again.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private string NothingFound
    {
        get
        {
            if (_results is not { Terms.Count: > 0 } results)
                return "Type a word or two the rules would use.";

            var words = string.Join(" or ", results.Terms);

            return _scope is { } chapter
                ? FormattableString.Invariant($"Nothing in Ch.{chapter.Chapter} uses {words}. Press Search to read the whole book.")
                : $"Nothing in the book uses {words}. Try the word the rules would use.";
        }
    }

    private static string Counted(int n, string one, string many) =>
        FormattableString.Invariant($"{n} {(n == 1 ? one : many)}");
}
